Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||10-15-2001||11:56 AM||||It failed suddenly on Sept 18th. I remember the date cause its my birthday.<P> had to tow the car home, 14 miles, with a rope - wifey was not amused driving the tow car.<P> the fuel pump was making a sputtering sound - I checked the relay and wireing first - that wasnt it.<P> did you know you cant siphon the tank through the gas filler neck? I do now! You have to pull the fuel lines off under the car. Gee I sure am glad I had filled the tank that morning - 10 miles before the fuel pump died.<P>  Taking the tank off the car I didnt have a 1/2" deep socket to get the nuts off, so I snapped the ends of the long bolts off - mental note: pick up somemore tank hanger T bolts when you get the fuel pump.<P> guess what?! nobody makes those cute little T shaped hanger bolts! Pontiac use to make them - but they dont sell Fiero parts anymore - dealers advice? "take some off a car in a junk yard!!!"<P> Got some 5/16" bolts at the HW store, cut them into the right shapes, and had a local welder fabricate them into replacement T bolts.<P> and the fuel pump I got from the autosupply store? then connectors didnt match - so I had to solder the wires to the terminals and cover them with heatshrink.<P> Oops! forgot to buy a 1/2" deep socket to put the T bolts back on with.<P> 4 weeks and about $120 later I have a new fuel pump and filter - car runs exactly the same now as it did 10 seconds before the last one quit.<P> But the new pump is a lot quieter at least.<P> Decided to NOT sell my 2000 saturn SL - having 4 cars for three drivers has turned out to be a good thing<P> esp is one of the cars is a 16 year old midengine sports car.<P> :c) BTW its great to have it back on the road again! driving around upstate NY in a two seater sports car on a crisp fall afternoon with the sunroof out:<P> Priceless!||||reg||11
